    "QuestionID": "P2-F-250",
    "question_state": "Certified",
    "Part2OnlyFlag": true,
    "UniqueConceptKey": "F-250-fraud-exposure-control-gap",
    "Stem": "Maya Caldwell, treasurer of Flash Logistics, is assessing fraud risk at a distribution hub where one clerk both receives customer cash payments and posts them to the accounts receivable ledger, creating the opportunity element of the fraud triangle. Maya estimates that through this control gap a dishonest clerk could divert 3% of the hub's weekly cash collections before detection. The hub collects an average of $250,000 in cash per week. Using a 52-week year, what is the estimated annual fraud exposure from this control gap?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "$7,500",
      "B": "$97,500",
      "C": "$390,000",
      "D": "$780,000"
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "C",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Annual exposure equals weekly collections times the diversion rate times 52 weeks: $250,000 × 3% = $7,500 per week, and $7,500 × 52 = $390,000. The control gap creates the fraud-triangle opportunity that this exposure quantifies.",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "The $7,500 figure is only the estimated diversion for a single week ($250,000 × 3%); it stops short of projecting the loss over the year, so it materially understates the exposure from a gap that persists for 52 weeks.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "The $97,500 figure equals the estimated diversion for about 13 weeks ($7,500 × 13), a quarterly view; the assessment horizon given in the scenario is a full 52-week year, so this choice understates the annual exposure.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "The $780,000 figure results from applying a 6% diversion rate ($250,000 × 6% × 52); the scenario specifies a 3% diversion estimate, so this choice overstates the expected annual exposure.",
    "Difficulty": "Easy",
    "DifficultyScore": 1,
    "CognitiveLevel": "Understand",
    "CalculationItem": true,
    "ItemStyle": "single-select",
    "LOSTag": "F.5",
    "FormulaReference": "",
    "CommonTrapReference": "Annualization trap (computing a weekly diversion figure and failing to project it over the full 52-week period)",
    "Authorities": [
      "COSO IC 2013"
    ],
