    "Part2OnlyFlag": true,
    "UniqueConceptKey": "customer-data-monetization-consent",
    "Stem": "Lena Fischer, a financial analyst at Flash Foods, is asked to help value a program that would sell aggregated customer purchase data to a third-party marketing firm. The data was collected under Flash Foods' published privacy policy, which states the information will be used only for internal loyalty analytics, and the proposal does not amend the policy or obtain customer consent. Under the IMA Statement of Ethical Professional Practice, what should Lena do?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Proceed with the sale, because the data is aggregated and no individual customer can be identified from it.",
      "B": "Proceed with the sale, provided the marketing firm signs a confidentiality agreement covering the data.",
      "C": "Decline to support the sale until Flash Foods amends its privacy policy and obtains customer consent for the new use of the data.",
      "D": "Proceed with the sale, because customer data is a business asset that Flash Foods may use as management directs."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "C",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under the IMA Statement's Confidentiality and Integrity Standards, customer information may be used only for the purposes for which it was collected and disclosed, so monetizing data gathered for loyalty analytics requires an amended privacy policy and customer consent.",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "Aggregation does not cure a use that contradicts the purpose disclosed at collection; even non-identifying summaries derive from data gathered under Flash Foods' promise of internal-only use, and privacy regimes such as GDPR and CCPA separately restrict repurposing data without consent.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "A confidentiality agreement with the buyer protects the data from onward redistribution, but it does not address the original use limitation; the core problem is Flash Foods' own repurposing of customer data beyond the stated purpose, which only an amended policy and customer consent can resolve.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "Ownership of the data does not override the commitments Flash Foods made in its privacy policy or the reasonable expectations of customers about how their data would be used; the IMA Integrity Standard prohibits actions that undermine trust even when management directs them.",
