    "UniqueConceptKey": "F-269-board-pack-material-item-suppression",
    "Stem": "Adaeze Onuorah, CFO at Flash Holdings, is assembling the quarterly board information pack. A division general manager asks her to omit a material customer-concentration analysis from the pack because the division expects to sign two new large customers within 60 days and the general manager believes the current concentration is temporary. The omitted analysis shows one customer is 28% of revenue and has been for four consecutive quarters. How should Adaeze analyze the request under the IMA framework?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Grant the omission, because the concentration is expected to be temporary and including it would needlessly alarm the board",
      "B": "Include the material concentration analysis with management's assessment, because the board has a right to relevant information that could influence its oversight decisions",
      "C": "Include only a verbal summary at the meeting so the board receives the information without it becoming part of the written record",
      "D": "Refer the request to the external auditor for a determination of whether the omission is acceptable"
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "B",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "A customer representing 28% of revenue for four consecutive quarters is a material concentration that the board needs to oversee business risk. Suppressing it from the board pack, even temporarily, deprives the board of relevant information and violates the IMA Credibility standard's duty to communicate all relevant information that could influence an intended user's understanding. The proper analysis is to include the analysis with management's forward-looking assessment, letting the board weigh the expectation of new customers against the observed concentration. The board's oversight role and the Credibility standard both require full and fair information flow.",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "The expectation that concentration is temporary does not justify suppressing material information from the board. The proper response is to present the concentration analysis together with management's assessment of the expected new customers, so the board can exercise oversight with full information rather than on a selectively filtered pack.",
