    "Part2OnlyFlag": true,
    "UniqueConceptKey": "records-retention-destruction-timing",
    "Stem": "Mariela Hoffmann, controller of Flash Manufacturing, a public issuer, is reviewing the records-retention schedule after a warehouse manager asks to destroy a batch of 8-year-old shipping documents to free storage space. General counsel confirms no litigation hold applies to the batch, and Mariela must decide whether the destruction may proceed now. Under the IMA Competence Standard and SOX §802, which action should Mariela take?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Destroy the records immediately because the company's retention schedule is the sole governing authority.",
      "B": "Destroy only records older than ten years and retain everything newer.",
      "C": "Wait until the current year-end audit is complete before destroying any records.",
      "D": "Confirm the batch is outside any statutory retention period and any active litigation hold, then allow destruction under the schedule."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "D",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "The IMA Competence Standard requires performing duties in accordance with relevant laws, regulations, and technical standards, and SOX §802 imposes minimum retention periods and criminalizes obstruction-related destruction, so destruction timing must respect statutory minimums, litigation holds, and the retention schedule.",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "The retention schedule is not the sole governing authority: SOX §802 and other statutes impose minimum retention periods for specified records, and an active litigation hold overrides the schedule, so Mariela must confirm those factors before any destruction is approved.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "A ten-year cutoff is an arbitrary age rule: destruction timing is driven by the applicable statutory retention minimums, any active litigation holds, and the organization's audit and record needs, so a fixed age threshold that ignores those governing factors could destroy records that must still be kept.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "Completion of the year-end audit is a practical checkpoint for audit-related records, but it is not a blanket condition on destroying every record class; the governing test is whether statutory retention periods and litigation holds permit destruction, not whether the audit calendar has closed.",
