    "Part2OnlyFlag": true,
    "UniqueConceptKey": "fraud-deterrence-unpredictable-rotated-surprise-audits",
    "Stem": "Maya Caldwell, treasurer and risk officer of Flash Foods, is designing a fraud-deterrence program for cash handling at the company's retail locations. Her goal is to maximize employees' perception that fraudulent behavior will be detected and punished. Which audit design element most effectively supports fraud deterrence?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Publish a fixed schedule of annual audits so every location knows exactly when its cash records will be examined.",
      "B": "Restrict surprise audits to a static list of locations that previously had exceptions, with no changes to timing or scope.",
      "C": "Conduct surprise audits only after a specific fraud allegation is received, so resources are not spent on unproven locations.",
      "D": "Rotate unpredictable timing, vary the locations and scope of unannounced checks, and communicate that surprise audits can occur at any location."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "D",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Fraud deterrence depends on a credible risk of detection; COSO IC 2013 monitoring activities include independent, unpredictable checks, so rotating the timing, locations, and scope of unannounced audits deters fraud more effectively than predictable or reactive-only audits.",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "Publishing a fixed schedule in advance removes the element of surprise and lets employees temporarily adjust records before examination, which undermines the deterrent effect that unannounced monitoring is designed to create under a strong control environment.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "Restricting surprise audits to a static list of previously flagged locations makes the program predictable and signals that unflagged locations will not be checked, reducing perceived detection risk and weakening fraud deterrence across the company.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "Performing surprise audits only after a specific allegation is received is reactive rather than preventive; effective fraud deterrence requires a credible, ongoing risk of unannounced review at any location, not audits triggered solely by complaints.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "",
