    "UniqueConceptKey": "ethical-leadership-culture-building-structural-levers",
    "Stem": "Naomi Castellanos, project manager at Flash Foods, leads a cross-functional team that has begun skipping quality checks to hit aggressive delivery dates, and several members joke that 'whatever it takes' is the team motto. Naomi wants to build a durable ethical team culture rather than rely on one-off messaging. Which approach best accomplishes that goal?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Deliver a one-hour ethics training session at the next team meeting and treat the culture objective as complete once attendance is recorded.",
      "B": "Publicly recognize only the members who hit every deadline and rely on individual accountability to police the quality of the work.",
      "C": "Ask the team to sign a values pledge and refer any future shortcut directly to human resources for discipline.",
      "D": "Model integrity in her own decisions, make ethical conduct an explicit performance-evaluation criterion, and maintain an anonymous channel for raising concerns without retaliation."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "D",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "A durable ethical culture requires leaders to model the standards, align incentives so integrity is evaluated alongside results, and give team members protected channels to raise concerns; these levers, consistent with the IMA Statement's integrity standard, change day-to-day operating norms rather than relying on ceremony or one-off messaging.",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "A single training session does not change day-to-day behavior because it lacks reinforcement: without leadership modeling, incentives tied to integrity, or protected reporting channels, attendance at one session leaves the 'whatever it takes' norm intact and gives no safe way for team members to surface shortcuts.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "Recognizing only deadline performers while leaving quality to individual accountability misaligns incentives and can normalize cutting corners; building an ethical culture requires rewarding integrity explicitly, holding every team member to the same standard, and providing accessible channels for raising concerns.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "A one-time pledge plus referral to discipline treats ethics as ceremony and punishment rather than an operating norm; durable culture requires ongoing leadership modeling, evaluation criteria that reward integrity, and non-retaliatory channels, so the pledge alone will not change how the team works day to day.",
