    "Topic": "F.307 uk-bribery-act-facilitation-prohibition",
    "QuestionID": "P2-F-307",
    "question_state": "Certified",
    "Part2OnlyFlag": true,
    "UniqueConceptKey": "uk-bribery-act-facilitation-payment-prohibition",
    "Stem": "Maya Caldwell, treasurer and risk officer of Flash Logistics, is reviewing the company's global anti-bribery program, which is aligned with both the UK Bribery Act and the FCPA. A country manager asks whether small expediting payments to customs officials to speed container clearance are acceptable under the UK Bribery Act. What is the correct treatment of facilitation payments under that Act?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "They are acceptable provided the amount falls below a de minimis threshold established by the local country office.",
      "B": "They are prohibited because the UK Bribery Act contains no exemption for facilitation payments.",
      "C": "They are acceptable whenever the payment secures only a routine governmental action such as customs clearance.",
      "D": "They are prohibited only when the recipient is a foreign public official acting in an official capacity."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "B",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "The UK Bribery Act criminalizes bribes intended to secure an improper advantage and, unlike the FCPA, contains no exception for facilitation payments, so the small expediting payments proposed for Flash Logistics are prohibited under that Act.",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "Choice A invents a de minimis monetary threshold that does not exist in the UK Bribery Act; the Act contains no monetary floor, so a low-value payment to expedite customs clearance remains a prohibited bribe even if the amount is small.",
