    "Topic": "F.313 second-opinion-external-consultant-scope",
    "QuestionID": "P2-F-313",
    "question_state": "Certified",
    "Part2OnlyFlag": true,
    "UniqueConceptKey": "second-opinion-external-consultant-scope",
    "Stem": "Priya Ramaswamy, senior segment analyst at Flash Foods, questions whether the company's capitalization of customer-acquisition costs understates expenses in the segment report. The CFO dismisses her concern, and Priya proposes obtaining a second opinion from an external accounting consultant. What is the appropriate scope for the second opinion?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Priya should describe the transaction only as favorably as possible so the consultant's opinion supports management's position and preserves internal harmony.",
      "B": "Priya should give the external consultant the complete, accurate facts of the transaction and the competing interpretations so the second opinion is objective and can support a fair conclusion.",
      "C": "Priya should obtain the second opinion only from a consultant who agrees in advance to reach the same conclusion as management.",
      "D": "Priya should rely on the consultant's opinion in place of her own professional judgment, since an external expert's view is conclusive on the accounting treatment."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "B",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "The Integrity standard requires communicating information fairly and objectively; a valid second opinion requires presenting the complete set of relevant facts, not a selective or pre-biased summary, to the external consultant.",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "Framing the transaction one-sidedly so the consultant reaches a predetermined conclusion violates the Integrity standard's requirement to communicate information fairly and objectively; a second opinion is only meaningful when built on complete, accurate facts.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "Selecting a consultant on condition that the conclusion match management's view converts the second opinion into a confirmation exercise and violates the requirement that professional judgment be communicated fairly and objectively under the Integrity standard.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "An external consultant's opinion is input into the member's professional judgment, not a substitute for it; the member remains responsible for concluding whether the treatment complies with the standards and must communicate that conclusion fairly.",
    "Difficulty": "Moderate-Easy",
    "DifficultyScore": 2,
    "CognitiveLevel": "Understand",
    "CalculationItem": false,
