    "QuestionID": "P2-F-315",
    "question_state": "Certified",
    "Part2OnlyFlag": true,
    "UniqueConceptKey": "ethics-hotline-confidentiality-balance",
    "Stem": "Maya Caldwell, risk officer at Flash Industrial, is designing the ethics hotline and must balance protecting reporter confidentiality with giving investigators enough information to act. Employees have said they will not use the hotline if they can be identified, while the compliance team warns that vague reports are unactionable. Which hotline design best achieves that balance?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Require every caller to identify themselves by name and department so that no report can be investigated without full attribution.",
      "B": "Assure callers of complete identity concealment and strip each report of all operational detail that could indirectly identify the reporter, so nothing in the report can be traced.",
      "C": "Route all hotline reports through the employee's immediate supervisor, who then decides what information, if any, to forward to the compliance function.",
      "D": "Allow anonymous reporting while asking callers to provide enough operational detail to permit investigation, and protect reporter identity by limiting access to reports and prohibiting retaliation."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "D",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "The Confidentiality standard requires safeguarding confidential information; a hotline that permits anonymous reporting, collects sufficient operational detail, and restricts access to reports balances reporter confidentiality with the organization's need to investigate.",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "Requiring every caller to identify themselves eliminates the confidentiality that encourages reporting of sensitive misconduct; effective hotline design permits anonymous reporting so employees can report fraud and abuse without fear of retaliation.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "Stripping all identifying detail leaves reports with too little information to investigate, and promising unconditional concealment cannot be honored once a formal inquiry begins; effective design collects sufficient operational detail and restricts access to the report.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "Routing reports through the immediate supervisor suppresses reporting when the supervisor is involved in the misconduct and undermines confidentiality; reports should go to an independent channel with access restricted to those who need the information to investigate.",
