    "Topic": "F.317 fcpa-foreign-official-gift-exception-scope",
    "QuestionID": "P2-F-317",
    "question_state": "Certified",
    "Part2OnlyFlag": true,
    "UniqueConceptKey": "fcpa-foreign-official-gift-exception-scope",
    "Stem": "Adaeze Onuorah, CFO/VP of Flash Industrial, is reviewing a bid for a large government contract in a foreign country. The regional sales director proposes including two items for the foreign procurement official: an engraved corporate pen set worth $40 and an all-expenses-paid week at a resort for the official's family. Under the FCPA, which action is appropriate for Adaeze?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Approve both items because gifts are lawful as long as they are recorded accurately in Flash Industrial's books and records.",
      "B": "Approve the modest pen set as customary business hospitality, but reject the resort vacation because it is a lavish personal benefit intended to influence the award.",
      "C": "Approve both items because any expenditure related to winning a foreign contract qualifies as a reasonable and bona fide business expense under the FCPA's promotional exception.",
      "D": "Reject both items because the FCPA prohibits giving anything of value to a foreign official under any circumstances."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "B",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under the FCPA anti-bribery provisions, only a reasonable, bona fide expenditure directly related to product promotion or contract performance, plus modest customary hospitality, is permissible; a lavish personal vacation given to influence an award is a prohibited bribe.",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "Accurate books and records do not legalize an otherwise corrupt payment; recording a lavish gift in the ledger does not convert a payment intended to influence the procurement award into a permissible expenditure, so keeping the books clean is not a defense.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "The FCPA's affirmative defense is narrow: the expenditure must be a reasonable, bona fide cost directly related to promoting, demonstrating, or explaining products or to performing a contract; an all-expenses-paid family resort vacation confers a personal benefit and falls outside that exception.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "The FCPA does not prohibit every gift; modest gifts of nominal value, customary business hospitality, and payments lawful under the foreign country's written laws are outside the anti-bribery prohibition, so a blanket rejection of both items overstates the statute's reach.",
    "Difficulty": "Moderate",
    "DifficultyScore": 3,
    "CognitiveLevel": "Apply",
    "CalculationItem": false,
    "ItemStyle": "single-select",
