    "question_state": "Certified",
    "Part2OnlyFlag": true,
    "UniqueConceptKey": "integrity-earnings-guidance-management",
    "Stem": "Priya Ramaswamy, senior investor-relations analyst at Flash Manufacturing, is drafting next quarter's earnings guidance. The realistic forecast, based on current orders and cost trends, is EPS of $1.85 on 4.0 million shares outstanding, but Adaeze Onuorah, CFO/VP, directs Priya to issue guidance of $1.60 so the company can beat the Street's expectation. What is the dollar amount by which the directed guidance understates the realistic forecast?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "$250,000",
      "B": "$500,000",
      "C": "$750,000",
      "D": "$1,000,000"
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "D",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Per-share gap = $1.85 - $1.60 = $0.25; $0.25 x 4,000,000 shares = $1,000,000. Issuing guidance knowingly below the realistic forecast to manufacture a later beat misleads investors and violates the Integrity standard of the IMA Statement of Ethical Professional Practice.",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "Using only 1.0 million shares instead of the 4.0 million shares outstanding gives $0.25 x 1,000,000 = $250,000; the $0.25 per-share understatement must be multiplied by the full 4.0 million shares, not one quarter of them.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "Multiplying the $0.25 per-share understatement by 2.0 million shares, or half the shares outstanding, yields $500,000; the correct computation multiplies the per-share gap by all 4.0 million shares to obtain $1,000,000.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "Multiplying the $0.25 per-share gap by 3.0 million shares gives $750,000, but Flash Manufacturing has 4.0 million shares outstanding, so the full understatement of guidance equals $0.25 x 4,000,000 = $1,000,000.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "",
    "Difficulty": "Moderate",
    "DifficultyScore": 3,
    "CognitiveLevel": "Apply",
    "CalculationItem": true,
    "ItemStyle": "single-select",
