    "UniqueConceptKey": "disabled-approval-control-data-integrity",
    "Stem": "During the month-end close at Flash Capital, Lena Fischer, a financial analyst, notices that the IT group removed the required second approver from the journal-entry workflow to shorten the close, and that several expense adjustments were posted using only her user ID in the audit trail. Lena reports the finding to Maya Caldwell, the risk officer, who confirms the system change but notes that no loss has occurred. What is the most appropriate analysis and response?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Treat the change as a control deficiency affecting data integrity, restore the dual-approval workflow, and investigate the unauthorized postings through the established escalation process.",
      "B": "Accept the change because a faster close is a legitimate efficiency gain and no fraud has been detected.",
      "C": "Reverse the unauthorized entries quietly and leave the new workflow in place, because the resulting balances are now correct.",
      "D": "Assign the IT administrator who made the change to review the affected postings, because the person who understands the system is best placed to validate them."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "A",
