    "QuestionID": "P2-F-333",
    "question_state": "Certified",
    "Part2OnlyFlag": true,
    "UniqueConceptKey": "confidentiality-nondisclosure-agreement-scope",
    "Stem": "Lena Fischer, a financial analyst at Flash Capital, received non-public customer margin data from Flash Industrial's finance team under a nondisclosure agreement signed for a potential acquisition. A portfolio manager notes that the NDA's definition of 'Confidential Information' lists only enumerated documents, namely the term sheet and audited financial statements, and argues the margin data therefore falls outside the NDA's scope and may be used in an unrelated deal memo. What should Lena do?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Use the margin data in the deal memo because the NDA covers only the documents it expressly enumerates.",
      "B": "Treat the margin data as confidential, use it only for the acquisition evaluation, and seek legal guidance before any other use.",
      "C": "Use the margin data because the NDA binds Flash Capital as an entity rather than Lena as an individual employee.",
      "D": "Refuse to use even publicly available information about Flash Industrial in the deal memo to avoid any appearance of impropriety."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "B",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under the IMA Confidentiality standard, information received in confidence stays confidential unless disclosure is authorized or legally required; NDA scope follows the parties' intent, so Lena must restrict use and confirm scope with counsel.",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "An enumerated-document list does not define the full scope of confidential treatment; information disclosed in confidence for the stated purpose remains protected even if the NDA's list omits it.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "Confidentiality obligations bind the entity and the employees who act for it; the fact that Lena is not a named signatory does not free her from the IMA Confidentiality standard.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "Refusing to use publicly available information exceeds the standard, which protects non-public information; over-restriction impairs analysis without advancing confidentiality.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "",
