    "UniqueConceptKey": "fcpa-foreign-subsidiary-supervision-duty",
    "Stem": "Adaeze Onuorah, CFO of Flash Holdings, is reviewing the compliance exposure created by a newly acquired foreign subsidiary whose sales agents have previously made payments to government officials to win contracts. She asks what duty the FCPA imposes on the parent company with respect to that subsidiary. Which statement correctly states the parent company's duty under the FCPA?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Because the subsidiary is a separate legal entity, the parent company has no responsibility for payments made by subsidiary personnel.",
      "B": "The parent must guarantee through quarterly audits that no improper payment has been made by the subsidiary at any time.",
      "C": "The parent satisfies its duty by including a compliance disclaimer in the subsidiary's annual financial statements.",
      "D": "The parent must exercise good-faith supervision and control over the subsidiary, and it can be held liable if it knew or should have known that improper payments were being made."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "D",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under the FCPA, an issuer is responsible for the acts of its foreign subsidiaries and must exercise good-faith supervision and control; knowledge of improper payments by subsidiary personnel can be imputed to the parent company.",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "Corporate separateness does not shield a parent under the FCPA; an issuer can be held responsible for the acts of its foreign subsidiaries and must supervise their compliance rather than treat them as unrelated entities.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "The FCPA does not demand a guarantee that no improper payment ever occurred; it requires reasonable, good-faith internal controls and oversight, and even a clean audit does not by itself discharge the parent's continuing supervision duty.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "A compliance disclaimer in the financial statements is not a substitute for supervision; the FCPA requires the parent to maintain adequate internal controls and exercise good-faith oversight over the subsidiary's sales practices.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "",
