    "UniqueConceptKey": "greenwashing-scope-1-scope-3-boundary",
    "Stem": "Lena Fischer, financial analyst at Flash Foods, is reviewing the company's new sustainability report, which claims that Flash Foods 'reduced its emissions' but discloses only Scope 1 and Scope 2 emissions. The report excludes emissions from the company's agricultural suppliers and from customer use of its packaged products. Which statement correctly interprets the emission boundary in this report?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Emissions from agricultural suppliers must be reported as Flash Foods' Scope 1 emissions because they are part of its value chain.",
      "B": "Supplier emissions and customer-use emissions are Scope 3 value-chain emissions of Flash Foods, so the 'reduced its emissions' claim is misleading unless the report states that it covers only Scope 1 and Scope 2.",
      "C": "Emissions from customer use of the company's products occur after the sale, so they fall outside all three scopes and may be omitted from the report.",
      "D": "To avoid greenwashing, Flash Foods must report all value-chain emissions as Scope 1, because only direct emissions are relevant to investors."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "B",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under the GHG Protocol scopes used in sustainability reporting, supplier and customer-use emissions are Scope 3 indirect value-chain emissions, so a total-emissions claim must disclose the Scope 1/Scope 2 boundary to avoid greenwashing, consistent with the integrity standard of the IMA Statement of Ethical Professional Practice.",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "Supplier emissions belong in the suppliers' own Scope 1 accounts; for Flash Foods they are indirect value-chain (Scope 3) emissions, so reporting them as Flash Foods' Scope 1 would misstate the boundary and mislabel the company's direct emissions.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "Customer-use emissions are not outside the scopes; product use is a recognized Scope 3 category, so omitting them from a total-emissions claim without stating the boundary can mislead stakeholders about Flash Foods' full carbon footprint.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "Classifying every value-chain emission as Scope 1 is incorrect; Scope 1 covers only emissions from sources Flash Foods owns or controls, while supplier and customer-use emissions belong in the Scope 3 categories that must be disclosed or excluded transparently.",
    "Difficulty": "Easy",
    "DifficultyScore": 1,
    "CognitiveLevel": "Understand",
