    "Part2OnlyFlag": true,
    "UniqueConceptKey": "ai-model-bias-exposure-quantification",
    "Stem": "Maya Caldwell, risk officer at Flash Capital, is reviewing a data-bias audit of the company's AI loan-approval model. This quarter the model reviewed 5,000 loan applications and approved 80% of all applicants, but it approved only 70% of the 2,000 applications submitted by members of a protected group. Flash Capital's average loan is $25,000, and the 80% approval rate is treated as the fair benchmark. What is the dollar volume of loans affected by the apparent bias this quarter?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "$12,500,000",
      "B": "$15,000,000",
      "C": "$5,000,000",
      "D": "$10,000,000"
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "C",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Bias-affected applicants = 2,000 protected-group applicants x (80% - 70%) = 200; 200 x $25,000 average loan = $5,000,000 of affected loan volume, which Maya must surface in the model-governance report under the fair and objective communication required by the IMA Statement of Ethical Professional Practice.",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "$12,500,000 wrongly applies the 10% bias gap to all 5,000 applicants (5,000 x 10% x $25,000); the gap must be applied only to the 2,000 protected-group applicants, because the other applicants were not subject to the differential treatment.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "$15,000,000 treats all 600 rejected protected-group applicants as bias-affected (600 x $25,000), but only the 200 excess rejections above the 80% benchmark (2,000 x 10%) represent the bias exposure.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "$10,000,000 multiplies the 20% overall rejection rate by the 2,000 protected-group applicants (2,000 x 20% x $25,000), which counts benchmark-expected rejections rather than the 200 excess rejections attributable to the bias gap.",
