    "Topic": "F.343 integrity-intercompany-transfer-pricing",
    "QuestionID": "P2-F-343",
    "question_state": "Certified",
    "Part2OnlyFlag": true,
    "UniqueConceptKey": "integrity-intercompany-transfer-pricing",
    "Stem": "Adaeze Onuorah, CFO of Flash Industrial, asks Mariela Hoffmann, the controller, to set intercompany transfer prices at levels that shift profit from a high-tax business unit to a low-tax subsidiary, describing the pricing as legitimate tax planning. Under the IMA Statement of Ethical Professional Practice, which action should Mariela take?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Agree, because reducing the group's tax burden is a legitimate objective that serves the shareholders.",
      "B": "Decline to set the artificial prices because the resulting charges would not reflect economic substance and would misstate operating results.",
      "C": "Set the requested prices but add a note that they are temporary and for tax purposes only.",
      "D": "Compromise by shifting only a small portion of the profit so that the distortion is immaterial."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "B",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "The IMA integrity standard requires mitigating conflicts of interest and refraining from conduct that would prejudice carrying out duties ethically; transfer prices set to shift profit rather than to reflect economic substance misstate the results of both units, so Mariela must decline to record them.",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "Tax minimization is a lawful objective, but the integrity standard does not permit achieving it by recording transactions that misstate performance; prices chosen solely to move profit between entities distort the reported results of both units and would be presented to users as though they were arm's-length charges.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "Adding a footnote does not cure the misstatement because the recorded intercompany charges still misstate segment profitability for the period; the IMA statement requires that information be communicated fairly and objectively, which artificial transfer prices, even ones labeled temporary, do not do.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "Shifting only a small portion of profit does not remove the ethical breach because the pricing still departs from economic substance to achieve a predetermined result; the integrity standard contains no immateriality exception for deliberately misstating intercompany charges.",
    "Difficulty": "Moderate-Easy",
    "DifficultyScore": 2,
    "CognitiveLevel": "Understand",
    "CalculationItem": false,
    "ItemStyle": "single-select",
    "LOSTag": "F.1",
    "FormulaReference": "",
