    "Topic": "F.345 competence-technology-change-management",
    "QuestionID": "P2-F-345",
    "question_state": "Certified",
    "Part2OnlyFlag": true,
    "UniqueConceptKey": "competence-technology-change-management",
    "Stem": "Flash Foods has migrated to a new enterprise resource planning system, and Adaeze Onuorah, CFO, asks Lena Fischer, a financial analyst, to certify that the monthly variance reports generated by the new system are accurate. Lena has not yet completed the required training on the new system and cannot verify how the system extracts and computes the data. Under the IMA Statement of Ethical Professional Practice, which action should Lena take?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Certify the reports because management is relying on her and the information technology group already tested the system.",
      "B": "Certify the reports but add a footnote stating that her training is pending.",
      "C": "Ask a coworker who completed the training to review the reports quietly, and then certify them herself.",
      "D": "Communicate her lack of training as a professional limitation and complete the training before certifying the reports."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "D",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "The IMA competence standard requires recognizing and communicating professional limitations or other constraints that would preclude responsible judgment or successful performance of an activity; Lena cannot responsibly certify reports whose underlying system logic she cannot verify, so she must communicate the limitation and complete the training first.",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "Reliance on the information technology group's testing does not substitute for Lena's own ability to perform her professional duty, because she cannot verify the accuracy of reports she is asked to certify; the competence standard requires her to recognize and communicate her own limitation rather than assume the system is correct.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "Certifying figures she cannot verify, even with a footnote about pending training, still amounts to performing the task beyond her current competence and leaves management with a certification that was not responsibly made; the competence standard requires communicating the limitation before performing the activity, not after.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "Having a coworker review the reports does not make Lena competent to certify them, and quietly substituting another person's work product avoids the required communication of her limitation; she must disclose the constraint and complete the training rather than obtain assurance indirectly.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "",
