    "QuestionID": "P2-F-346",
    "question_state": "Certified",
    "Part2OnlyFlag": true,
    "UniqueConceptKey": "f346-tone-at-the-middle-management-conduct",
    "Stem": "At Flash Manufacturing, project manager Naomi Castellanos observes that two production supervisors routinely approve timecards with padded shift hours, and the plant's senior managers never question the practice. A team lead asks Naomi whether reporting accurate hours still matters when \"everyone does it.\" Under the company's ethics program, what should Naomi do to support an ethical culture?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Personally insist on accurate timecards in her own area and hold the supervisors accountable, recognizing that middle managers set the ethical example employees actually follow.",
      "B": "Tell the team lead that only senior executives are responsible for setting the ethical tone, so the practice is outside her authority to address.",
      "C": "Take no action because the overstated hours are immaterial in amount and will not affect the year-end financial statements.",
      "D": "Advise the team lead to wait for the confidential hotline to catch the issue, since anonymous reporting is the only sanctioned channel."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "A",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Choice A is correct. COSO IC 2013's control-environment principle requires management at all levels, including middle managers such as Naomi, to demonstrate and reinforce integrity and ethical values; her insistence on accurate timecards and accountability for the supervisors models the tone at the middle. The IMA Statement's Integrity standard likewise requires refusing to participate in or condone the misreporting of hours.",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "Senior executives set the overall tone, but middle managers are not relieved of responsibility for accurate reporting; COSO IC 2013's control environment holds every level of management accountable for modeling and enforcing ethical values, so deferring entirely to executives would leave the padded timecards unaddressed.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "Immateriality of the amounts does not justify tolerating deliberate misreporting; COSO IC 2013 emphasizes that integrity and ethical values apply regardless of dollar size, and condoned padding of timecards normalizes dishonest conduct that can grow into larger misstatements over time.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "The hotline is a valuable reporting channel, but it does not replace Naomi's day-to-day responsibility to model accurate behavior and correct misconduct she directly observes; passively waiting for an anonymous complaint is inconsistent with the control environment's emphasis on demonstrated commitment to ethical values.",
    "Difficulty": "Moderate",
    "DifficultyScore": 3,
