    "Topic": "F.363 whistleblower-program-metrics-effectiveness",
    "QuestionID": "P2-F-363",
    "question_state": "Certified",
    "Part2OnlyFlag": true,
    "UniqueConceptKey": "whistleblower-program-effectiveness-metrics",
    "Stem": "Maya Caldwell, risk officer at Flash Logistics, is asked by the audit committee to judge whether the company's whistleblower program is effective. Over the past year, tip volume fell 40%, median investigation time rose, and the substantiation rate increased. Maya notes that no employee survey has measured awareness of the hotline and that two retaliation complaints were resolved only after legal intervention. What should Maya conclude about the program's effectiveness?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "The program is more effective than before, because fewer tips with a higher substantiation rate show that reporting quality improved.",
      "B": "The program's effectiveness cannot be assessed from tip volume and substantiation alone; Maya should weigh awareness, reporter trust, investigation quality, and retaliation outcomes before concluding.",
      "C": "The program is less effective, because falling tip volume is the clearest sign that employees no longer trust the channel.",
      "D": "The rising substantiation rate shows investigators are rejecting valid tips, so the program's intake process should be redesigned."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "B",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "SOX §806 prohibits retaliation against employees who report suspected wrongdoing and protects the reporting channel a whistleblower program exists to encourage; assessing effectiveness therefore requires evidence of awareness, trust, and retaliation outcomes, not tip volume or substantiation rate alone.",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "A higher substantiation rate with falling volume could reflect better screening, but it could also reflect underreporting or fear of retaliation; volume and substantiation alone cannot establish that the program has become more effective.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "Tip volume is a noisy indicator; declines can follow improved prevention, reduced misconduct, or diminished awareness, so treating volume as the decisive measure of employee trust overstates the meaning of a single metric.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "A rising substantiation rate more plausibly indicates that tips are being screened or investigated more effectively, not that valid reports are being rejected, so redesigning intake on that inference is unsupported by the data.",
    "Difficulty": "Very Difficult",
    "DifficultyScore": 5,
    "CognitiveLevel": "Evaluate",
    "CalculationItem": false,
    "ItemStyle": "single-select",
