    "QuestionID": "P2-F-364",
    "question_state": "Certified",
    "Part2OnlyFlag": true,
    "UniqueConceptKey": "vendor-evaluation-objectivity-hospitality",
    "Stem": "Lena Fischer, financial analyst at Flash Capital, leads the due-diligence review of Meridian Supply, a vendor bidding for a large outsourcing contract. Meridian's CFO has paid for two expensive dinners and now offers Lena courtside playoff tickets, remarking that the review is on track. Lena is confident the hospitality will not affect her judgment. Which response best protects Lena's objectivity under the IMA Statement of Ethical Professional Practice?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Accept the tickets and disclose them to management, because disclosure eliminates any appearance of impropriety.",
      "B": "Decline the tickets but continue accepting routine meals, because customary business courtesies do not threaten objectivity.",
      "C": "Decline the hospitality, inform Meridian that the evaluation will be decided on merit, and report the offers to her supervisor, recusing herself if the relationship appears to compromise the process.",
      "D": "Accept the hospitality and complete the review quickly, documenting in the file that the gifts did not influence her recommendation."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "C",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "The IMA Statement's Integrity standard requires members to refuse any gift, favor, or hospitality that would appear to influence their actions; declining the hospitality, putting the vendor on notice, and reporting the offers, with recusal if needed, protects objectivity.",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "Disclosure reduces but does not eliminate the conflict; the Integrity standard requires refusing hospitality that would appear to influence actions, so accepting the tickets even with disclosure still violates the standard.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "Repeated meals at a vendor's expense can reasonably appear intended to influence the review, and the Integrity standard bars any gift, favor, or hospitality that would appear to influence actions, not merely tickets.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "Self-serving documentation that the gifts had no influence does not satisfy the Integrity standard, which requires refusing the hospitality outright rather than accepting it and then asserting continued impartiality.",
