    "UniqueConceptKey": "fraud-triangle-quota-revenue-recognition-escalation",
    "Stem": "At Flash Manufacturing, sales orders surge in the final week of each quarter as the sales team races to meet quotas tied to large bonuses. Controller Mariela Hoffmann finds that several large orders were booked as revenue before customers accepted delivery, with side letters permitting return. Applying the fraud triangle, how should Mariela evaluate the situation and respond?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "The quota bonus is the incentive/pressure element and the weak revenue-cutoff controls are the opportunity element, so Mariela should escalate the improper bookings through the appropriate reporting channels without delay.",
      "B": "The early bookings are acceptable under the matching principle because the goods were shipped before quarter-end, so Mariela should record a disclosure note and take no corrective action.",
      "C": "Because no employee has admitted wrongful intent, the fraud triangle is not satisfied, so Mariela should treat the bookings as a training issue and require sales staff to sign an annual ethics acknowledgment.",
      "D": "The division should increase quota bonuses so salespeople can earn more through legitimate sales, reasoning that larger incentives will remove the pressure to stretch the revenue cutoff."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "A",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under the IMA Statement of Ethical Professional Practice and the COSO IC 2013 fraud-risk assessment, fraud requires incentive/pressure, opportunity, and rationalization; the quota bonus and weak cutoff controls satisfy the first two, obligating Mariela to report the misstatement.",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "",
