    "QuestionID": "P2-F-381",
    "question_state": "Certified",
    "Part2OnlyFlag": true,
    "UniqueConceptKey": "competence-outsourcing-oversight-duty",
    "Stem": "Mariela Hoffmann, controller of Flash Manufacturing, plans to outsource monthly account reconciliations to a third-party provider whose staff hold no accounting credentials and have limited U.S. GAAP experience. The provider's bid is materially cheaper, and CFO Adaeze Onuorah wants the handoff completed before quarter end. Under the IMA Competence standard, which action must Mariela take before relying on the provider's output?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Delegate the reconciliation to the provider and rely on its internal quality-control procedures without further review, since the outsourcing contract shifts responsibility for the work.",
      "B": "Evaluate the provider's qualifications, arrange needed training, and supervise and review the provider's work so the output is competently prepared.",
      "C": "Use the provider only for data entry and certify the reconciliations without examining the underlying work papers.",
      "D": "Instruct the provider to follow its own procedures and treat any errors as the provider's legal liability alone."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "B",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under the IMA Competence standard, a member must perform duties in accordance with relevant laws, regulations, and technical standards and provide accurate, clear, concise, and timely information; outsourcing does not transfer that duty, so Mariela must assess the provider's skills, arrange training, and review the results. (B)",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "This is incorrect because the Competence standard requires the member to ensure work is performed in accordance with relevant technical standards; contract terms do not transfer Mariela's professional responsibility to verify the provider's qualifications and to review the output.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "This is incorrect because certifying reconciliations without examining the work papers means Mariela cannot ensure the information is accurate, clear, and timely as the Competence standard requires; limited delegation still demands review and oversight of the preparer's work.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "This is incorrect because shifting error liability to the provider does not cure a competence failure; the IMA standards hold Mariela accountable for the decision support information she provides, and she must monitor outsourced work even when a contract allocates legal risk.",
    "Difficulty": "Moderate",
    "DifficultyScore": 3,
    "CognitiveLevel": "Apply",
    "CalculationItem": false,
    "ItemStyle": "single-select",
