    "QuestionID": "P2-F-383",
    "question_state": "Certified",
    "Part2OnlyFlag": true,
    "UniqueConceptKey": "integrity-inventory-count-misstatement",
    "Stem": "At Flash Foods' year-end, the physical count of a raw-materials SKU totals 52,000 units at a cost of $9.00 per unit, but the plant manager instructs the counters to override the recorded quantity to 58,000 units. Controller Mariela Hoffmann discovers the override and is told to leave it as is. If the overstated count stands, what is the effect on ending inventory, cost of goods sold, and pre-tax income?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Ending inventory is understated by $54,000 and cost of goods sold is overstated by $54,000.",
      "B": "Ending inventory and pre-tax income are each overstated by $27,000.",
      "C": "Ending inventory is overstated by $54,000, while cost of goods sold and pre-tax income are unaffected.",
      "D": "Ending inventory is overstated by $54,000, cost of goods sold is understated by $54,000, and pre-tax income is overstated by $54,000."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "D",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "The override adds 6,000 units (58,000 minus 52,000) at $9.00 per unit, or $54,000, to recorded ending inventory; because goods available for sale is unchanged, cost of goods sold is understated by $54,000 and pre-tax income is overstated by the same $54,000. The Integrity standard bars conduct that subverts the organization's legitimate objectives, so the override must be refused and escalated. (D)",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "This is incorrect because recording more units than physically counted inflates, not reduces, ending inventory, and an inflated ending inventory lowers cost of goods sold under the periodic inventory equation; the direction of both effects in this choice is reversed.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "This is incorrect because the misstatement is 6,000 units at $9.00 per unit, or $54,000, not $27,000; the full amount flows through ending inventory and pre-tax income in the same period, with no halving of the effect between accounts.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "This is incorrect because the misstatement cannot be confined to the balance sheet: with goods available for sale unchanged, the $54,000 overstatement of ending inventory necessarily understates cost of goods sold and overstates pre-tax income by the same amount.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "",
