    "Part2OnlyFlag": true,
    "UniqueConceptKey": "incentive-compensation-ethical-design",
    "Stem": "Flash Holdings is redesigning its executive bonus plan, and CFO Adaeze Onuorah must evaluate the proposal. The proposed plan ties 80% of each bonus to a single quarterly EBITDA growth target, with no clawback and no independent review of reported results. Which design feature would most effectively reduce the incentive for managers to manipulate reported earnings?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Increase the bonus payout percentage so managers are less tempted to manipulate earnings for a marginal gain.",
      "B": "Use multi-period performance measures that balance financial and nonfinancial metrics, and add clawback provisions with independent review of reported results.",
      "C": "Shorten the performance period to monthly targets so manipulation can be detected and corrected earlier.",
      "D": "Pay the bonus entirely in stock options so managers benefit only from long-term share price performance."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "B",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "An ethical incentive design rewards multi-period performance, balances financial and nonfinancial measures, and includes governance safeguards such as clawback provisions and independent review of reported results, so managers cannot profit from short-term earnings manipulation, consistent with the IMA Statement's requirement of fair and objective decision support. (B)",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "This is incorrect because increasing the payout size raises the payoff from manipulation and can intensify, rather than reduce, the incentive to overstate earnings; compensation ethics require design safeguards, not larger rewards tied to the same risky metric.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "",
