    "BlueprintDomain": "Professional Ethics",
    "Topic": "F.405 credibility-materiality-threshold-judgment",
    "QuestionID": "P2-F-405",
    "question_state": "Certified",
    "Part2OnlyFlag": true,
    "UniqueConceptKey": "credibility-materiality-threshold-quantitative-qualitative",
    "Stem": "Treasurer Maya Caldwell is finalizing Flash Foods' quarterly earnings release and finds that one marketing contract was misclassified as an asset instead of an expense. The misstatement is only 0.3% of net income, well below the company's 5% quantitative materiality threshold. Maya must decide whether the error is disclosed in the earnings release. Given that the misclassification changes the presentation of results and affects the trend of reported expenses, what is the appropriate application of the materiality standard?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Do not disclose the error because 0.3% of net income is far below the 5% quantitative materiality threshold and therefore immaterial by definition.",
      "B": "Disclose the error only if the Securities and Exchange Commission staff has previously commented on this specific account in a past filing.",
      "C": "Ignore the error because it arises from a routine classification judgment, and routine classification judgments do not rise to the level of material misstatements.",
      "D": "Disclose the error because materiality is a matter of professional judgment that considers both quantitative size and qualitative factors, and the misclassification affects trend presentation and how the results are communicated."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "D",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Materiality is a matter of professional judgment under SEC Staff Accounting Bulletin No. 99 (SAB 99) and the IMA Statement of Ethical Professional Practice, so the 0.3% misstatement must be disclosed because the misclassification affects the presentation of results and the trend of reported expenses, a qualitative factor that keeps the error material despite the small size.",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "A bright-line 5% threshold does not by itself make the error immaterial because materiality is a matter of professional judgment under SAB 99, and qualitative factors such as the effect on trend presentation and how results are communicated can make a small misstatement material.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "Prior SEC staff comments are not a prerequisite for disclosure because materiality is judged from the perspective of a reasonable investor under SAB 99, so the qualitative impact of the misclassification requires disclosure even if no prior comment letter addressed the account.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "Routine classification judgments can still be material because materiality is a matter of professional judgment under SAB 99, and an error that changes how results and trends are presented is material even when the underlying decision appeared routine.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "",
    "Difficulty": "Moderate",
