    "Stem": "Priya Ramaswamy, the senior investor-relations analyst at Flash Holdings, prepared a segment earnings forecast based on adjusted operating income, a non-GAAP metric, that the company uses in investor communications. She now learns that a major customer has announced plans to terminate its contract, which materially changes the forecast, but management wants to delay any revision until the termination is contractually final to avoid market disruption. Which of the following is the best course for Priya to recommend?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Priya should recommend remaining silent until the termination is contractually final, because premature disclosure could expose Flash Holdings to legal liability for misleading investors.",
      "B": "Priya should promptly inform the CFO and, consistent with company policy, support a timely revision or withdrawal of the forecast, because the IMA Credibility standard requires disclosing all relevant information that could reasonably be expected to influence an intended user's understanding.",
      "C": "Priya should revise the forecast for internal planning only and leave investor communications unchanged, because external disclosure is the responsibility of the investor-relations department.",
      "D": "Priya should revise the forecast to reflect favorable developments but omit the customer loss, because downside risks are inherently uncertain and need not be communicated."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "B",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "The IMA Credibility standard requires disclosing all relevant information that could reasonably be expected to influence an intended user's understanding, and because the forecast is a non-GAAP metric used in investor communications, SEC Regulation G also requires that the disclosure not be misleading, so a timely revision or withdrawal is required.",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "A is wrong because withholding a known material change to avoid market disruption conflicts with the IMA Credibility standard, which requires disclosing relevant information that could reasonably influence an intended user's understanding; exposure is managed through timely, accurate disclosure, and delaying a revision while the forecast circulates is itself misleading.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "C is wrong because the IMA Credibility standard applies to all reports, analyses, and recommendations regardless of the audience; keeping investor communications unchanged while revising internal figures would mislead external users and conceal a material change that Priya is obligated to disclose.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "D is wrong because the Credibility standard requires fair and objective communication of all relevant information, including unfavorable developments; selectively disclosing favorable items while omitting the customer loss misrepresents the forecast and violates the duty to communicate information fairly and objectively.",
    "Difficulty": "Very Difficult",
    "DifficultyScore": 5,
    "CognitiveLevel": "Evaluate",
    "CalculationItem": false,
