    "QuestionID": "P2-F-425",
    "question_state": "Certified",
    "Part2OnlyFlag": true,
    "UniqueConceptKey": "board-selection-objectivity-diversity-integrity",
    "Stem": "The board nominating committee of Flash Holdings must fill a vacant seat, and the CEO privately urges Adaeze Onuorah, the CFO who chairs the committee, to appoint his longtime friend, who shares the CEO's views. The CEO calls the company's stated commitment to board diversity mere optics, while one director argues that any diversity consideration would compromise merit-based selection. Which of the following best reflects the ethically sound approach for the committee?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "The committee should appoint the CEO's preferred candidate, because a cohesive board that agrees with the CEO acts more decisively and the CEO is accountable to shareholders for results.",
      "B": "The committee should ignore demographic and experiential diversity entirely, because true merit-based selection requires evaluating candidates solely on financial and operational credentials.",
      "C": "The committee should adopt a diversity target only if it can be measured and reported under a recognized ESG framework, because disclosure requirements are the only ethical driver for board composition.",
      "D": "The committee should evaluate candidates against a broad set of qualifications, including diversity of background and perspective, while maintaining independence from management influence, because board integrity requires objective and conflict-free selection rather than tokenism or homogeneity."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "D",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Board integrity requires independent, objective director selection that weighs the full range of qualifications, including diversity of background and perspective, while guarding against management influence, consistent with the IMA principles of integrity and objectivity in governance.",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "A is wrong because board members owe fiduciary duties to shareholders and must exercise independent judgment; appointing the CEO's personal ally to ensure agreement subordinates board integrity to management influence and undermines objective oversight of the company.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "B is wrong because considering diversity of background and perspective is compatible with merit; integrity in board selection requires objective evaluation of the full range of qualifications, and excluding diversity factors entirely can entrench groupthink and management capture in the boardroom.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "C is wrong because the ethical case for board quality does not depend on a reporting framework; although ESG frameworks can support transparency, board integrity requires independent and objective director selection whether or not diversity metrics are externally disclosed.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "",
