    "UniqueConceptKey": "F-426-competence-decline-unqualified-assignment",
    "Topic": "F.426 competence-standard-declining-unqualified-work",
    "Stem": "Flash Manufacturing analyst Maya Patel, Analyst, is asked by Flash's Controller to present a complex derivative valuation to the board next week. Maya has no training in derivative valuation and Flash has a qualified treasury analyst available. Under the IMA Statement of Ethical Professional Practice, what should Maya Patel do?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Decline the assignment, explain the competence gap, and recommend the qualified treasury analyst present the valuation",
      "B": "Accept the assignment and learn derivative valuation quickly before the board presentation to show initiative",
      "C": "Accept the assignment but ask the Controller to review her slides briefly before the meeting",
      "D": "Postpone the board meeting until Maya completes a short online course on derivatives"
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "A",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under the IMA Competence standard, management accountants must maintain expertise and perform duties per professional standards, disclosing limitations. Maya lacks derivative valuation competence; accepting would violate the standard and expose the board to unreliable analysis. The correct action is to decline, disclose the gap transparently, and recommend a qualified professional. Board decisions require current competence, not aspirational learning. Business interpretation: Analyst Maya Patel should escalate to the Controller that the treasury analyst owns the topic, preserving credibility and protecting Flash from misstatement. Common trap is confusing willingness to learn with current competence or believing supervisory review cures a competence gap.",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "Choice B confuses willingness to learn with current competence; the misconception treats rapid self-study as satisfying the standard, which ignores that Competence requires demonstrable expertise at the time duties are performed, not a plan to acquire it before the board presentation.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "Choice C omits that supervisory review cannot cure the competence gap; the trap assumes the Controller's brief review makes an unqualified presentation acceptable, while the standard requires the individual to refuse work beyond expertise and reassign to the qualified analyst.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "Choice D understates the depth of competence required; the error treats one short course as conferring reliable derivative valuation expertise, while the standard demands appropriate, proven capability when the board relies on the analysis for decisions.",
    "Difficulty": "Easy",
    "DifficultyScore": 1,
    "CognitiveLevel": "Remember",
